  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nHigh pressure will nudge south today and allow for dry conditions to \npersist across the area into Saturday morning before an active \nweather pattern returns for the weekend and into early next week. \nInitially on Saturday, a low pressure system is expected to push \neast towards the southern Great Lakes region, moving a warm front \nnorth across much of the Ohio Valley. This warm front is expected to \nstall somewhere south of the Lake Erie lakeshore, acting as a path \nfor the aforementioned low pressure to move along. Given the \npositioning of this system, ample moisture is expected to stream \nacross much of the region. This increase in moisture coupled with a \nfew mid-level shortwave troughs pushing east near that stalled \nboundary should result in periods of showers. Models continue to \nsuggest the period of heaviest rain should occur late Saturday into \nSunday. Given high PWAT values, deepening warm cloud layers, and a \nskinny CAPE profile in modeled soundings the primary concern with \nthese showers will be very efficient and heavy rainfall. The current \nforecast suggests rainfall over 1 inch across much of northern Ohio \nwith 0.8-1 inch expected in northwest Pennsylvania. This very heavy \nrainfall has the potential to result in flooding concerns, \nespecially if the rainfall is prolonged or impacts the same area \nmultiple times.  To highlight this concern WPC has highlighted areas \nalong and west of I71 in a Day 2 & 3 Slight (2 of 4) ERO with the \nremainder of the area in a Marginal (1 of 4) ERO. The threat for \nsevere weather remains limited across the CWA and should remain \nisolated to along and south of the warm front which should stay just \nsouth of the area. \n\nAfter Sunday, the pattern looks to remain unsettled as a broad upper \nlevel trough continue to have embedded shortwaves move along it. \nWith each push of shortwave energy, additional rainfall chances will \noccur. Models are in a bit of disagreement in timing of these \nvarious shortwaves, but long range models are now suggesting this \npattern persisting through at least mid-week. This prolonged period \nof multiple rounds of precipitation could further worsen the flood \npotential across the area given the expected wet antecedent \nconditions from this weekend. Will have to continue to monitor the \ntrends in models along with the trends in hydrologic parameters to \ndetermine if the heavy rain potential will stick around. \n\nGiven the placement of the CWA in relation to the aforementioned \nboundary, temperatures today will be mild in the low to mid 70s \nbefore gradually cooling over the weekend with highs only expected \nto reach into the low to mid 60s on Monday. Overnight temperatures \nwill follow a similar trend with lows beginning the period in the \n60s before cooling into the 50s next week.",
